<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  Laptop-side FastDDS profile for talking to the TMR with NATIVE (default) discovery.

  Assumes multicast works on the laptop<->robot Ethernet link (direct cable / dumb switch,
  or a managed switch with multicast/IGMP enabled). Verify first:
    (robot)  ros2 multicast receive
    (laptop) ros2 multicast send      # robot should print the received message

  This laptop keeps WiFi up for internet, so it is multi-homed. This profile pins DDS to
  the Ethernet NIC (interfaceWhiteList) so it uses/advertises ONLY 172.16.16.140 - but it
  keeps DEFAULT (SIMPLE, multicast) discovery on that interface. No discovery server, no
  initial-peers, and NO config needed on the robot.

  If you use a direct cable and disable WiFi during teleop, you may not even need this file
  (plain RMW=rmw_fastrtps_cpp + ROS_DOMAIN_ID=0 is enough). Keep it while WiFi stays on.

  Update <address>172.16.16.140</address> if the laptop Ethernet IP changes
  (ip -4 addr show | grep 172.16.16) - though tmr_laptop_env.sh renders this address fresh
  from the live IP into /tmp/tmr_fastdds_laptop_<uid>.xml on every source, so this file's
  own placeholder value is template-only and rarely needs hand-editing.

  maxMessageSize=65500 and the 127.0.0.1 whitelist entry (added 2026-08-30) mirror
  render_dds_profile.sh's profile, which diagnosed the same gap for the Docker camera path
  on 2026-08-24: without maxMessageSize, FastDDS's default UDP fragmentation limit is too
  small for camera frames, so image topics DISCOVER fine (small control-plane messages) but
  deliver zero payload - and 127.0.0.1 keeps same-host viewer/recorder traffic flowing even
  if the wired link drops mid-session. This profile was written for small GELLO/pedal
  control messages only and never needed either until cameras started sharing it natively.

  Usage (laptop):
    export RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_fastrtps_cpp
    export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=0
    export FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=$PWD/configs/fastdds_laptop_discovery.xml
    unset ROS_DISCOVERY_SERVER
-->
<dds>
  <profiles xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
    <transport_descriptors>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>eth_udp</transport_id>
        <type>UDPv4</type>
        <maxMessageSize>65500</maxMessageSize>
        <interfaceWhiteList>
          <address>172.16.16.140</address>
          <address>127.0.0.1</address>
        </interfaceWhiteList>
      </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>

    <participant profile_name="laptop_eth" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <userTransports>
          <transport_id>eth_udp</transport_id>
        </userTransports>
        <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
      </rtps>
    </participant>
  </profiles>
</dds>
